<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n40769" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">rĕdundantĭa</span></b> ,
		ae, f.
		redundans, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>an overflowing</i>, <i>superfluity</i>, <i>excess.</i> *
			</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">aër crassus, qui non habet crebras
					redundantias,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Vitr. 1,
					6 <i>med.</i></span></i>
			— </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>, <i>redundancy</i>, <i>excess</i>, of language, feeling, etc.: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">illa pro Roscio juvenilis redundantia,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Or. 30, 108</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">benignitatis,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Tert. Apol. 31</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">carere redundantiā et egestate,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">App. Dogm. Plat. 2, 5</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
